## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where users resolving exec-backed SecretRefs could get inherited Object prototype members treated as provider response entries when the requested id matched names such as `toString`.

## Why This Change Was Made

Exec provider responses now check `values` and `errors` with own-property membership before accepting an id. The change is scoped to exec response parsing and keeps the existing missing-id and per-id error behavior.

## User Impact

Users and operators get deterministic SecretRef failures when an exec provider omits a requested id, including ids that collide with Object prototype names, instead of accidentally reading inherited values.

## Real Behavior Proof

Behavior addressed: an exec SecretRef provider can omit a requested id named `toString`; the resolver must treat that as a missing provider response entry, not as an inherited Object prototype member.

Environment: local checkout of this PR branch `alix/secrets-exec-own-ids`, Node.js via the repository `tsx` loader. The provider was a real executable shell script created in a private temp directory with mode `0700`; it read stdin and returned the real exec-provider protocol payload `{"protocolVersion":1,"values":{}}` on stdout.

Command run after this patch:

```bash
node --import tsx --input-type=module <<'EOF'
import fs from "node:fs/promises";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { resolveSecretRefString } from "./src/secrets/resolve.ts";

const root = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "openclaw-exec-secretref-proof-"));
await fs.chmod(root, 0o700);
const provider = path.join(root, "missing-toString-provider.sh");
await fs.writeFile(
  provider,
  [
    "#!/bin/sh",
    "cat >/dev/null",
    "printf '%s' '{\"protocolVersion\":1,\"values\":{}}'",
  ].join("\n"),
  { mode: 0o700 },
);
await fs.chmod(provider, 0o700);

const config = {
  secrets: {
    providers: {
      liveexec: {
        source: "exec",
        command: provider,
        passEnv: ["PATH"],
      },
    },
  },
};

const ref = { source: "exec", provider: "liveexec", id: "toString" };
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  proof: "exec SecretRef provider missing inherited id",
  providerScript: provider.replace(root, "<tmp-proof-dir>"),
  providerOutput: { protocolVersion: 1, values: {} },
  requestedRef: ref,
}, null, 2));

try {
  await resolveSecretRefString(ref, { config, env: { PATH: process.env.PATH ?? "" } });
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ result: "unexpected-success" }, null, 2));
  process.exitCode = 1;
} catch (error) {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    result: "expected-failure",
    errorName: error?.name,
    errorMessage: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),
  }, null, 2));
  if (!(error instanceof Error) || !error.message.includes('response missing id "toString"')) {
    process.exitCode = 1;
  }
}

await fs.rm(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
EOF
```

Observed output:

```json
{
  "proof": "exec SecretRef provider missing inherited id",
  "providerScript": "<tmp-proof-dir>/missing-toString-provider.sh",
  "providerOutput": {
    "protocolVersion": 1,
    "values": {}
  },
  "requestedRef": {
    "source": "exec",
    "provider": "liveexec",
    "id": "toString"
  }
}
{
  "result": "expected-failure",
  "errorName": "SecretRefResolutionError",
  "errorMessage": "Exec provider \"liveexec\" response missing id \"toString\"."
}
```

This exercises the real exec-provider path without mocking `spawn`: OpenClaw starts the configured provider command, sends the protocol request over stdin, parses stdout, then resolves the requested `exec:liveexec:toString` SecretRef. After the patch, the missing `toString` id fails deterministically as a missing response id.

What was not tested: no external secret manager or private credential store was contacted; the provider script returned only an empty protocol response and no secret material.

## Evidence

- `git diff --check`
- `node scripts/test-projects.mjs src/secrets/resolve.test.ts` (27 tests passed)
- Live exec SecretRef proof above: real executable provider returning an empty response for requested id `toString` now fails with `Exec provider "liveexec" response missing id "toString".`
- Pre-PR collision check: no open PRs touched `src/secrets/resolve.ts` or `src/secrets/resolve.test.ts`; no open PR covered exec response id inherited-key handling.

## CI Note

The only observed CI failure at the time of this update was `checks-node-compact-small-whole-2`, timing out in `src/tui/tui-pty-local.e2e.test.ts > creates and adopts a fresh session through the real Gateway backend`. That shard does not touch `src/secrets/resolve.ts` or `src/secrets/resolve.test.ts`; the focused secrets test and the full secrets project test passed locally.
